What It Is
A plugin host and index for all modules (Apps 1 through 23). It provides the module registry, the shared schema registry, the shared provenance database, the shared authentication layer, the shared reporting layer, the unified search, and the launcher. It does not duplicate functionality. It is the generalization of App 14 (the STAG plus Null-Model Combined Workbench) to all 23 apps. It is for research groups, labs, consortia, and anyone who uses more than one module.

Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Host all 23 modules (Apps 1 through 23) as plugins.

Provide a module registry that lists the available modules, their versions, their dependencies, and their entry points.

Provide a shared schema registry that holds the STAG schema (Artifact 2, Appendix B.1), the STAG SHACL schema (Artifact 2, Appendix B.3), the STAG codebook (Artifact 2, Appendices A.1 through A.11), the Null-Model Protocol schema (from App 8 and App 9), and the deposit schema (from App 23).

Provide a shared provenance database that holds the provenance records for all modules. It reuses the provenance database (App 20) and extends it with the module identifier.

Provide a shared authentication layer that authenticates users and authorizes access to modules and records.

Provide a shared reporting layer that produces reports across modules.

Provide a unified search that searches across all modules (works in the library, STAG annotations, Null-Model Protocol results, partitions, nodes, events, models, revisions, domain annotations, audit log entries).

Provide a launcher that opens any module in its own view.

Provide a workspace that lets a user open multiple modules side by side.

Provide a navigation that lets a user move between modules.

Provide a status view that shows the health of each module (running, stopped, error).

Provide a configuration view that lets an administrator configure each module.

Provide a logging view that aggregates logs from all modules.

Provide a plugin interface that allows new modules to be added.

Provide a version compatibility check between modules and the shared core.

Provide a health check for the shared core and each module.

Provide a backup and restore for the shared core and each module.

Provide an audit log for every operation across all modules.

Provide a command-line interface for administration.

Provide a web interface for users and administrators.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic: same inputs produce same outputs.

Fast: launch a module in under two seconds; search across all modules in under three seconds.

Scalable: support up to 10^6 records across all modules.

Versioned: the master records the version of each module and the version of the shared core.

Reproducible: the master records the provenance for every operation.

Portable: works on Linux, macOS, and Windows.

Well-documented: module registry documentation, plugin interface documentation, and user guide.

Input Specification
Input 1. Module registry file. Required. A JSON file listing the available modules, their versions, their dependencies, and their entry points.

Input 2. User credentials. Required for authenticated operations.

Input 3. Module identifier. Required for module-specific operations.

Input 4. Configuration file. Required for the shared core and each module.

Input 5. Plugin package. Optional. A new module to add to the registry.

Input 6. Search query. Required for unified search.

Input 7. Workspace file. Optional. A file describing a user's workspace (which modules are open, their layout, their state).

Input 8. Backup file. Optional. A file containing the shared core's state and each module's state.

Input 9. Output format. Optional. Values: json (default), text, html.

Output Specification
Output 1. Module registry. JSON. The list of available modules, with their versions, dependencies, and entry points.

Output 2. Module status. JSON. The health of each module (running, stopped, error).

Output 3. Unified search result. JSON. The list of records matching the search query, across all modules.

Output 4. Workspace. JSON or HTML. The user's workspace, with the modules open and their state.

Output 5. Configuration view. JSON. The configuration of the shared core and each module.

Output 6. Logging view. JSON or text. The aggregated logs from all modules.

Output 7. Audit log entry. JSON. The operation, the module, the record, the user, the timestamp, and the result.

Output 8. API response. JSON. The result of the API operation, with HTTP status.

Output 9. Plugin interface documentation. Markdown or HTML. The interface a new module must implement.

Output 10. Backup file. A JSON archive or a database dump.

Output 11. Reproducibility record. JSON. The version of each module, the version of the shared core, the configuration, and the timestamp.

Module-by-Module Specification
Module 1: Module Registry
Holds the module registry. Each entry has:

module_id (string, required)

module_name (string, required)

module_version (string, required)

module_description (string, optional)

module_dependencies (array of strings, optional)

module_entry_point (string, required)

module_api_endpoint (string, optional)

module_ui_route (string, optional)

module_status (running, stopped, error)

module_configuration_schema (JSON, optional)

module_health_check (string, optional)

The registry is loaded from the module registry file at startup.

Module 2: Shared Schema Registry
Holds the shared schemas. It is the union of the STAG schema registry (App 17) and the Null-Model Protocol schema registry (App 18), plus the deposit schema (App 23). Provides lookup by schema name and version.

Module 3: Shared Provenance Database
Holds the provenance records for all modules. It reuses the provenance database (App 20) and extends it with the module identifier. Each provenance record has:

provenance_id (string, primary key)

module_id (string, foreign key to module_registry)

record_type (work, annotation, result, partition, node, event, model, revision, domain_annotation, other)

record_id (string)

user_id (string)

user_affiliation (string or null)

timestamp (ISO8601)

source (string)

tool_version (string)

operation (create, read, update, delete, query, aggregate, search, export, import, moderate, backup, restore)

created_at (ISO8601)

Module 4: Shared Authentication Layer
Provides authentication (API keys, OAuth, or JWT) and authorization (role-based access control: reader, depositor, moderator, admin, and module-specific roles). The authentication layer is shared across all modules.

Module 5: Shared Reporting Layer
Provides reporting across modules. Supports reports that combine data from multiple modules (for example, a report that shows a work from the library alongside its STAG annotations and its Null-Model Protocol results).

Module 6: Unified Search
Provides unified search across all modules. Searches works in the library, STAG annotations, Null-Model Protocol results, partitions, nodes, events, models, revisions, domain annotations, and audit log entries. Returns the results grouped by module.

Module 7: Launcher
Provides a launcher that opens any module in its own view. The launcher reads the module registry, presents the available modules, and opens the selected module.

Module 8: Workspace
Provides a workspace that lets a user open multiple modules side by side. The workspace state (which modules are open, their layout, their state) is saved to a workspace file.

Module 9: Navigation
Provides navigation between modules. A sidebar lists the available modules; clicking a module opens it in the workspace.

Module 10: Status View
Provides a status view that shows the health of each module. The status view reads the module status from the module registry and the health check results.

Module 11: Configuration View
Provides a configuration view that lets an administrator configure each module. The configuration is stored in a configuration file per module.

Module 12: Logging View
Provides a logging view that aggregates logs from all modules. The logs are collected from each module's log file or log endpoint.

Module 13: Plugin Interface
Provides a plugin interface that allows new modules to be added. A module must implement the following interface:

registerModule() returns the module's metadata (module_id, module_name, module_version, module_description, module_dependencies, module_entry_point, module_api_endpoint, module_ui_route, module_configuration_schema, module_health_check).

startModule(configuration) starts the module.

stopModule() stops the module.

healthCheck() returns the module's health (running, stopped, error).

getApiEndpoints() returns the module's API endpoints.

getUiRoutes() returns the module's UI routes.

getProvenanceRecords() returns the module's provenance records.

getAuditLog() returns the module's audit log entries.

backup() returns the module's state.

restore(state) restores the module's state.

Module 14: Version Compatibility Checker
Checks that each module's version is compatible with the shared core's version. If a module is incompatible, the master refuses to load it and reports the incompatibility.

Module 15: Health Check
Provides a health check for the shared core and each module. The health check runs periodically and on demand. The results are displayed in the status view.

Module 16: Backup and Restore
Provides backup and restore for the shared core and each module. Backup produces a JSON archive or a database dump. Restore loads the backup.

Module 17: Audit Logging
Logs every operation across all modules. Each audit log entry has:

audit_id (string, primary key)

operation (create, read, update, delete, query, aggregate, search, export, import, moderate, backup, restore)

module_id (string, foreign key to module_registry)

record_type (string)

record_id (string)

user_id (string)

timestamp (ISO8601)

result (success, failure)

details (JSON)

created_at (ISO8601)

